# Overview
#
# A back-reference points back at an earlier passage without naming it
# (`as mentioned above`), so the reader must search for the passage. The
# fix links the section that the phrase means or drops the phrase. The
# rule sits at Required, because it matches exact phrases and the fix is
# mechanical.
#
# Expression
#
# `verbs` lists the verbs of saying, such as `mentioned` and
# `explained`. `as` and one of those verbs count before `above`,
# `earlier`, `before`, or `previously`, and after `previously` or
# `already` (`as already noted`). `as we` and a verb such as `saw` or
# `discussed` count before one of the same words (`as we saw earlier`).
# `as mentioned`, `as noted`, `as stated`, and `as discussed` also count
# with a glued comma.
#
# `aforementioned`, `above-mentioned`, and `previously mentioned` point
# back the same way. A space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so the
# `above` branch names a hyphen and a space between its two words. It
# then matches the hyphenated and the spaced spelling.
#
# False Positives
#
# Before a source, the phrase cites the source (`as stated in RFC
# 9110`), so it passes. `as previously described in RFC 9110` still
# counts, and the rule accepts that rare misfire.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProsePositionalReference reports the same verbs without `as`
# (`described earlier`, `outlined below`). Its guard skips a verb that
# sits right after `as`, so `as described earlier` draws one finding
# from the two rules. The guard reads only the word before the verb, so
# `as we discussed earlier` draws a finding from each rule.
#
# ProseChangeNarration reports `previously` before a comma, so `As
# described previously,` draws a finding from that rule too, and no
# guard separates the two.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 41 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled
# hits pointed back without naming the passage. The 2 misfires linked
# the section in the same sentence (`As described above in Container
# restart policy`).
expr verbs [mentioned, noted, stated, discussed, described, explained, outlined, covered]
expr earlier [above, earlier, before, previously]
expr main [
    (as @verbs @earlier),
    (as [previously, already] @verbs),
    (as we [saw, discussed, noted, mentioned, explained] @earlier),
    (as [mentioned, noted, stated, discussed],),
    aforementioned,
    (above[-, ( )][mentioned, noted]),
    (previously mentioned)
]
let message "Back-reference. Name or link what it points at, or delete the phrase."
let description "Flags `as mentioned above` and similar phrases that point back without naming the passage."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The header is parsed as stated in RFC 9110."
allows "The limit is five, as noted in the changelog."
